
Radiation can be easily visualized. In fact, it was first visualized by Rutherford in his experiment addressing the nature of the nucleus. His experiment concluded that atoms are made of a dense, positively-charged core surrounded by the electron cloud. In his experiment, alpha particles were used as bullets impacting the atoms. An analysis of the distribution of the alpha particles emerging from the collision led to his conclusion. In order to detect the emerging particles, Rutherford and his students were visually counting the particles as flashes—counts of light—were produced when an alpha particle struck a screen with zinc sulphide, a scintillating material. These observations required the concentrated attention of the observer, and hence, it was tedious and not that accurate.
